WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.670
<v Narrator>So now let's discuss advanced concepts</v>

2
00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.280
of Aurora that you need to know going into the exam.

3
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.710
So let's talk about replica auto-scaling.

4
00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.390
So let's say we have a client

5
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.390
and we have three Aurora instances right now.

6
00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:15.690
So one will be writing through the writer endpoint

7
00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.460
and the other two would be reading

8
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.620
through the reader endpoint, okay?

9
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.200
Now let's say that we are having many, many requests,

10
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.660
read requests on the reader endpoint,

11
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.210
and so therefore, the Amazon Aurora databases

12
00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.520
have an increased CPU usage.

13
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.370
In that case, we can set up replica auto-scaling.

14
00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.440
And what this will do is that it will add

15
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.840
obviously Amazon Aurora replicas,

16
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.120
and then what will happen is that automatically,

17
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.730
the reader endpoint is going to be extended

18
00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.580
to cover these new replicas.

19
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:45.900
And therefore, these new replicas

20
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.880
will start receiving some traffic

21
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.310
and the reads will be happening

22
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:51.930
in a more distributed fashion,

23
00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.750
hopefully bringing down the overall CPU usage.

24
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.553
So this is replica auto-scaling.

25
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.150
The second thing is custom endpoints.

26
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.520
So let's say we have the same setup,

27
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.000
but this time, we have two different kinds of replicas.

28
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.120
As you can see, we have some db.r3.large

29
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.970
and some db.r5.2xlarge.

30
00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.840
So some read replicas are bigger than others.

31
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.370
And the reason you would do this

32
00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:21.120
is that you wanna define a subset of your Aurora instances

33
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.010
as a custom endpoint.

34
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.320
So let's say we define a custom endpoint

35
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.860
on these two bigger Aurora instances.

36
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.470
The reason we would do so is, for example, that we know

37
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:34.470
that these instances are more powerful

38
00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.150
and therefore they're going to be better

39
00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.380
to run analytical queries on these specific replicas.

40
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.550
So when you do so,

41
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.770
you have a custom endpoint that's defined.

42
00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.630
And when you have a custom endpoint,

43
00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.060
generally the reader endpoint itself

44
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.520
is not used after defining custom endpoints.

45
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.940
So it would not disappear, but you would not use it anymore.

46
00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.630
And what you would do, like practically speaking,

47
00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.970
is set up many custom endpoints

48
00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.130
for many different kind of workloads,

49
00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:03.750
therefore allowing you to query

50
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:07.083
only a subsets of your Aurora replicas.

51
00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:09.960
Next, we have serverless.

52
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.310
So this is going to give you

53
00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.710
automated database instantiation

54
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.470
and auto-scaling based on actual usage,

55
00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.830
which is great when you have infrequent, intermittent,

56
00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.840
or unpredictable workloads.

57
00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.870
And therefore, you don't need to do any capacity planning.

58
00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.110
You're going to pay per the second of each Aurora instance

59
00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.400
that is being spun up,

60
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.650
and this can be a lot more cost-effective.

61
00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:32.700
So how does that work?

62
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.860
Well, the client is going to talk

63
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.620
to a proxy fleet that is managed by Aurora.

64
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.370
And in the backend, many Aurora instances will be created

65
00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.860
based on your workload in a serverless fashion,

66
00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:48.270
so you don't have to provision capacity at all in advance.

67
00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.700
Finally, we have Global Aurora.

68
00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.170
So in case we have Aurora Cross Region Read Replica,

69
00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.390
it's going to be very helpful for disaster recovery.

70
00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:58.860
Very simple to put in place,

71
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.710
but you can also set up the Aurora Global Database,

72
00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.810
which is the recommended way of doing things today.

73
00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.820
In this case, you have one primary region

74
00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.980
where all the reads and writes happen.

75
00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.760
You can set up up to 10 secondary read-only regions

76
00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.610
where the replication lag should be less than one second

77
00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:19.050
and up to 16 read replicas per secondary region.

78
00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.360
And this will help you decrease latency

79
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.370
for the read workloads all over the world,

80
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.940
but also in case you have a database outage in one region.

81
00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.700
Promoting another region for disaster recovery purposes

82
00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.430
has an RTO, so recovery time objective,

83
00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:33.300
of less than one minute,

84
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.320
so it takes less than one minute

85
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.090
to recover into another region.

86
00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.010
And here is a sentence you should look out for

87
00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:39.570
from an exam perspective,

88
00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.000
it takes on average less than one second

89
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.890
to replicate data across region

90
00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.140
for your Aurora Global Database.

91
00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.880
So this is something that if you see in the exam,

92
00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.970
it's a hint to use a Global Aurora.

93
00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.050
So let's have a look.

94
00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.060
We have us-east-1 as our primary region,

95
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.700
which is where the applications do the read and writes,

96
00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.850
and then we'll set up a secondary region in eu-west-1

97
00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.320
or some replication happening

98
00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.630
with a global database of Aurora.

99
00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.590
And the applications there can do read-only from that setup.

100
00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.600
But in case us-east-1 fails,

101
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.610
then we can failover to eu-west-1

102
00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:15.450
by promoting it as a read-write Aurora cluster.

103
00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.280
Aurora also has an integration

104
00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.380
with machine learning services within AWS.

105
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.120
So the idea with Aurora Machine Learning

106
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.520
is that you can have ML-based predictions

107
00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.830
to your applications via the SQL interface.

108
00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.960
It's a simple, optimized, and secure integration

109
00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.800
between Aurora and different AWS machine learning services.

110
00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.200
So two supported services are SageMaker,

111
00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.310
which allows you to use

112
00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.590
any kind of machine learning model in the backend,

113
00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:40.740
or Amazon Comprehend, if you want to do sentiment analysis.

114
00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:41.700
Now, you don't need to be an expert

115
00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.320
in SageMaker or Comprehend.

116
00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:44.250
You just need to know that

117
00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.530
Aurora has an integration with those.

118
00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.260
So in order to use Aurora Machine Learning,

119
00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.690
you don't need to have a machine learning experience.

120
00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.990
And the use cases for this would be to have fraud detection,

121
00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.580
ads targeting, sentiment analysis,

122
00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.400
or product recommendation all within Aurora.

123
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.650
So to give you the architecture idea,

124
00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:03.270
Aurora is going to be connected

125
00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:06.150
to the machine learning services in AWS.

126
00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:09.150
And your application can just run a very simple SQL query.

127
00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.820
For example, what are the recommended products?

128
00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.800
Aurora will send some data

129
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.600
into the machine learning services,

130
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.280
such as, for example, the user's profile,

131
00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:18.990
the shopping history, et cetera, et cetera.

132
00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.000
And then the machine learning service

133
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.430
will return a prediction directly to Aurora.

134
00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.940
For example, the user should buy a red shirt and blue pants,

135
00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.430
and then Aurora can just return the query results

136
00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:30.810
to the application,

137
00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.410
all through as a result of the SQL query,

138
00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:35.580
which is very handy.

139
00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:39.240
So let's talk about Babelfish for Aurora PostgreSQL.

140
00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.290
So this is a feature that allows Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL

141
00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:44.730
to understand commands

142
00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.820
that are targeted for Microsoft SQL Server

143
00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:49.470
using the language T-SQL.

144
00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:51.270
So let me explain so it's clearer.

145
00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:54.810
If you have an application in your company today

146
00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:57.750
that's using Microsoft SQL Server as a database,

147
00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.090
then your applications are going to be written

148
00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.530
and they're going to use a driver

149
00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:04.020
called the SQL Server Client Driver,

150
00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:05.610
which is going to send the commands

151
00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:08.970
to your Microsoft SQL Server using T-SQL.

152
00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.280
But tomorrow, you want to migrate to the cloud.

153
00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.680
And so you migrate to Aurora PostgreSQL,

154
00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.410
which is a scalable database in the cloud that you like,

155
00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.230
but the engine is PostgreSQL.

156
00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:22.710
So that means that your applications must be converted

157
00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.810
to using the PostgreSQL driver.

158
00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.090
And the SQL language is a little bit different.

159
00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.850
It's using the PL/pgSQL language.

160
00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:30.748
So it's just a different language.

161
00:06:30.748 --> 00:06:32.550
You don't need to remember the name of it.

162
00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.830
So the idea is that how do we make sure

163
00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:39.480
that our SQL Server application does not have to be migrated

164
00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:40.800
to be rewritten?

165
00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:41.633
For this, we use Babelfish

166
00:06:41.633 --> 00:06:45.870
and Babelfish is going to be enabled on Aurora PostgreSQL,

167
00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.390
and this allows your application

168
00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:51.330
to just communicate to Aurora PostgreSQL

169
00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.360
using the T-SQL language through Babelfish.

170
00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.400
And so there's almost nothing to change.

171
00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.470
So that means that your SQL Server applications

172
00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:00.900
can now work on Aurora PostgreSQL

173
00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:05.370
with little to no code changes using the same driver.

174
00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:06.690
And so this is very handy

175
00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.060
because well, migrations are super easy.

176
00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.880
Now to do the migration, you use something called,

177
00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.440
and we'll see this later on this course,

178
00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.850
something called AWS SCT and DMS to migrate from,

179
00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.820
for example, SQL Server to Aurora PostgreSQL.

180
00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:25.020
But Babelfish is here to do the translation of T-SQL queries

181
00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.930
into Aurora PostgreSQL.

